uation."], "tags": ["intransitive"]}, {"examples": [{"text": "John won't be here for a while because he's working out.", "bold_text_offsets": [[44, 55]], "type": "example"}, {"text": "Wow, you're looking good! Do you work out?", "bold_text_offsets": [[33, 41]], "type": "example"}, {"text": "Alright, here we go! / Five, four, three, two, and one / Are you ready to work out?", "ref": "2018, “She Works Out Too Much”, in Little Dark Age, performed by MGMT:", "type": "quotation", "bold_text_offsets": [[74, 82]]}], "senseid": ["en:exercise"], "categories": ["English intransitive verbs", "English terms with quotations", "English terms with usage examples"], "links": [["exercise", "exercise"]], "raw_glosses": ["(intransitive) To exercise, especially by lifting weights."], "glosses": ["To exercise, especially by lifting weights."], "tags": ["intransitive"]}, {"examples": [{"text": "To work out your core", "bold_text_offsets": [[3, 11]], "type": "example"}, {"text": "Hot, worked-out body and a creative mind possessed by this hunky, handsome, married guy.", "ref": "1990 December 9, “Personal advertisement”, in Gay Community News, volume 18, number 21, page 12:", "type": "quotation", "bold_text_offsets": [[5, 15]]}], "categories": ["English terms with quotations", "English terms with usage examples", "English transitive verbs"], "links": [["strengthen", "strengthen"]], "raw_glosses": ["(transitive) To strengthen a part of one’s body by exercise."], "glosses": ["To strengthen a part of one’s body by exercise."], "tags": ["transitive"]}, {"examples": [{"text": "The two Russians made good farmhands, and in summer they worked out together.", "ref": "1918, Willa Cather, chapter 5, in My Ántonia, Boston: Houghton Mifflin, page 38:", "type": "quotation", "bold_text_offsets": [[57, 67]]}, {"text": "[…] with them good wages, maybe a fella can get hisself a little piece a land an’ work out for extra cash.", "ref": "1939, John Steinbeck, chapter 13, in The Grapes of Wrath, New York: Vikin